WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.270
We all fall down but John Wick always seems
to get back up.

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:04.730
Is his suit magic?

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:07.460
And who's listening in on Assassin Radio,
anyway?

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:08.960
Spoilers for John Wick 4

00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:13.510
As surprising as it may seem especially for
a franchise as successful and lucrative as

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:17.289
the "John Wick" series is, everyone's favorite
dog-loving assassin appears to die at the

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:18.930
end of "John Wick: Chapter 4."

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:23.170
After Wick refuses to shoot his old friend
Caine in a duel, both to protect Caine's daughter

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:27.449
and to take out the arrogant Marquis Vincent
de Gramont, Caine's bullet wounds John critically.

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:31.913
"You come here thinking there is a way out
for you, Mr. Wick.

00:00:33.977 --> 00:00:35.190
There is not."

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.190
Though the wound doesn't kill John immediately,
it seems to be the final blow to John as he

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.969
succumbs to the many other injuries that he's
gotten across the rest of the film.

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:45.620
From there, the movie cuts to the Bowery King
and Winston.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.510
They look over John's tombstone, laid to rest
next to the grave of his wife, Helen.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:51.430
But is John actually dead?

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.480
After all, we don't see him being buried.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.150
Furthermore, Winston could've helped fake
John's death so he could finally live in peace.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.650
And they could use the Bowery King as a legitimate
witness.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.899
Maybe his "goodbye" is knowing that John wouldn't
be able to see him again.

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:06.979
To be fair, we're hoping Wick gets to find
peace one way or another.

00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:10.930
It makes sense narratively and thematically
to kill him off, and frankly, more franchises

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.727
could use definitive endings.

00:01:12.727 --> 00:01:16.740
At the end of "John Wick: Chapter 4," right
before the ending credits roll, we see the

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.890
Bowery King and Winston pay their last respects
to John.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.479
Before leaving, however, Winston puts his
hand lovingly on John's gravestone, tearfully

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:28.450
stating "goodbye, my son," while showcasing
a tattoo of John's family crest on his finger.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.416
Does this mean that Winston was John Wick's
biological father?

00:01:31.416 --> 00:01:33.400
"I don't sit at the table."

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.864
"Your family does."

00:01:34.864 --> 00:01:38.370
Winston and John's being related by blood
seems unlikely.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:42.119
For one, John is canonically an orphan, and
nothing implies that Winston gave him up as

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:42.885
a baby.

00:01:42.885 --> 00:01:46.719
It's more likely that Winston simply saw John
as an adopted son rather than just another

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:48.329
faceless assassin.

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:52.479
Perhaps Winston even raised John, which, if
so, could be a subplot for the upcoming "John

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:54.430
Wick" spin-off series "The Continental."

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:58.350
We know that John was raised by the Ruska
Roma, but since Winston's tattoo is of the

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.830
same crest, he could've been a part of his
upbringing as well.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:05.725
However, it is most likely Winston saying
"goodbye, my son" was a sign of respect and mourning.

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:09.628
But John's mysterious parentage is still intriguing
to think about nonetheless.

00:02:09.885 --> 00:02:13.660
The Marvel Cinematic Universe has had a profound
and lasting effect on Hollywood.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.370
One of the things it helped popularize is
the inclusion of a post-credits scene.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.260
Obviously, the MCU didn't invent these little
teaser moments, but Marvel has certainly helped

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.349
popularize their inclusion regardless.

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:27.780
After the credits roll on "John Wick: Chapter
4," we see Caine about to be reunited with

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.780
his long-lost daughter after John Wick's sacrifice
during their climactic duel.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.650
This led to the High Table sparing Caine and
Caine's daughter's lives.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.450
However, earlier in the film, while Caine
was still chasing Wick down, the blind assassin

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:42.420
got into a deadly sword fight with another
one of Wick and Caine's old allies, the manager

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.360
of the Osaka Continental Hotel, Shimazu Koji.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.330
Caine ended up killing Koji in front of his
daughter, Akira.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.980
As a result, In the final scene, Akira finds
Caine and attacks him before he can reach

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:53.805
his daughter.

00:02:53.805 --> 00:02:56.885
We don't see the fight itself, so several
questions remain.

00:02:56.885 --> 00:02:59.904
Did she fight and kill him, or will something
or someone intervene?

00:02:59.904 --> 00:03:03.260
Is she or Caine the new face of the "John
Wick" franchise?

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.379
Only time will tell.

00:03:04.678 --> 00:03:09.000
One of the main appeals of the "John Wick"
series, besides its crazy action, stunts,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.650
and surprisingly intricate lore and world-building,
is the fact that the cast is always full of

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.010
great actors playing the colorful and eccentric
assassins that John encounters, both as friend

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:17.854
or foe.

00:03:17.854 --> 00:03:21.970
This includes Willem Dafoe, Halle Berry, Anjelica
Huston, and Lawrence Fishburne.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.530
And "John Wick: Chapter 4" is no exception.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:28.790
Besides Hong Kong legend Donnie Yen and current
horror icon Bill Skarsgård, the sequel also

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:33.150
introduces the Harbinger, played with intriguing
intensity by Clancy Brown.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.069
What's interesting about the Harbinger, is
that one of the first things we see of him

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:39.670
is his finger's been cut off, similar to what
happened to John Wick in "John Wick: Chapter

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:40.989
3 — Parabellum."

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:44.489
In that film, Wick cuts off his finger to
create a blood oath to the High Table which

00:03:44.489 --> 00:03:46.189
frees him of the bounty on his head.

00:03:46.189 --> 00:03:50.200
He was also supposed to kill Winston, which
he ultimately refused to do.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.250
But is Brown's Harbinger someone who fulfilled
his oath to the High Table?

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:57.239
Maybe that's why he's so obsessed with the
rules, especially "the old ways," and often

00:03:57.239 --> 00:04:00.358
talks back to his apparent superior, the corrupt
Marquis.

00:04:00.358 --> 00:04:03.710
Because of this detail, it seems like the
Harbinger is a foil for John.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:08.181
His path seems like a route John might've
taken had he not left the assassin lifestyle behind.

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:12.580
At the end of the previous film, Winston betrays
John, who had just risked his life to protect

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:17.079
Winston and his hotel against an army of highly
trained, armor-wearing High Table gunmen.

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:21.199
Nonetheless, when all is said and done, Winston
takes out a small pistol and shoots John multiple

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:24.120
times and knocks him off the rooftop of the
Continental.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.270
This is because the Adjudicator deconsecrated
Winston's hotel.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.949
"Gentlemen, this institution has now been
deconsecrated.

00:04:29.949 --> 00:04:32.669
Business may now be conducted on Continental
grounds."

00:04:32.669 --> 00:04:36.110
The menacing voice of the High Table gave
Winston the choice to get his hotel back if

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:37.300
he killed John first.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:42.340
Luckily for John, after falling multiple stories
off a rooftop, he miraculously survives.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.800
He's saved by the Bowery King and the two
plot revenge.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.650
However, when John meets Winston again in
"John Wick: Chapter 4," there seems to be

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:52.360
little bad blood outside of oblique references
to the past film.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:55.229
Wick even comments that Winston "always has
an angle."

00:04:55.229 --> 00:04:58.889
John seems to trust Winston enough to agree
to his plan to set up a one-on-one duel with

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:03.510
the Marquis to escape the High Table and "coincidentally"
get the Continental back.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.740
There's definitely a bond between the two
and maybe even some understanding from John

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.080
about the tough situation Winston was in,
but it makes little sense why he wouldn't

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.980
be angrier, and possibly vengeful, about Winston's
act of betrayal.

00:05:14.454 --> 00:05:18.630
"John Wick: Chapter 4," like most of the other
films in the series, is an intense globe-trotting

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:19.697
action thriller.

00:05:19.697 --> 00:05:24.724
Wick travels from New York to the Middle East
to Japan to New York again to Berlin to Paris,

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:28.479
all in an effort to escape the High Table
and kill anyone who gets in his way.

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:32.880
The problem is, the High Table is nigh-omnipresent
and incredibly wealthy and well-connected.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.770
It seems extremely unlikely that Wick could
easily board a transcontinental plane or ship

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.070
without being identified immediately.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:41.319
Or at least it wouldn't be so easy that he
could do it multiple times throughout the

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:43.130
film without any trouble.

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:46.880
Wick is a highly trained assassin, but that's
because he's extremely proficient at fighting,

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.180
marksmanship, and using improvised weapons.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:53.300
He's renowned for his prowess at killing,
not disguises, deception, or subterfuge.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:58.090
In fact, like James Bond, he's always pretty
easily recognized by fellow assassins, despite

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.170
that being quite an occupational hazard.

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.710
Obviously, airports are awash in surveillance
and security measures.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:07.750
Even if Wick has the requisite fake passports
and other falsified documents, the "John Wick"

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:11.229
series has shown time and time again that
assassins are everywhere and that their tech

00:06:11.229 --> 00:06:13.560
and resources rival that of world governments.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:17.900
All that being said, it's quite possible that
in "John Wick: Chapter 4," John's last ally,

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.930
Koji, grants him one last favor and gives
him safe passage back to New York.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:25.940
Toward the end of "John Wick: Chapter 4,"
before the climactic duel between Wick and

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:30.909
Caine, there is an extended action scene that
pits John against a throng of thugs throughout Paris.

00:06:30.909 --> 00:06:35.300
This includes a car chase where Wick drives
a door-less muscle car, engages in a fistfight

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.960
during the middle of high-speed traffic at
the Arc de Triomphe, and a continuous shot

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.180
of John Wick blowing away thugs with a shotgun
in an abandoned building.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:46.800
In fact, John encounters the most foes at
once in the entire film during this section.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.110
That's because the Marquis has arranged for
a radio station to send a broadcast out to

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:54.050
all the assassins in Paris about the incredible
bounty on John's head.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:58.259
The radio station itself is located in the
Eiffel Tower, with a DJ somehow keeping tabs

00:06:58.259 --> 00:07:00.569
on Wick's whereabouts throughout the fight.

00:07:00.569 --> 00:07:03.730
This does raise the question, is this an assassins-only
broadcast?

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:07.930
Or is it something a random person could tune
into if their antenna got the right frequency?

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:12.580
While it's safe to assume the station is only
for assassins, that isn't ever made explicitly

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.630
clear in the film.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:17.270
The members of the guild seem to use special
radios that could be encrypted, but it's never

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.949
addressed specifically.

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:22.669
If the radios aren't encrypted, there's the
possibility the station is normally a standard

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:27.129
broadcast that is sometimes forced to do the
bidding for the High Table from time to time.

00:07:27.129 --> 00:07:30.180
The organization certainly has its fingers
in many pies, after all.

00:07:30.849 --> 00:07:33.307
John Wick is best known for his prowess at
killing.

00:07:33.307 --> 00:07:37.039
He spends most of the series' runtime killing
countless goons and other highly trained assassins

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:38.039
with ease.

00:07:38.039 --> 00:07:41.940
The only thing that gives away his humanity
is that he sometimes seems a bit winded afterward,

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:45.110
with a few bloody scratches on his face and
blood stains on his suits.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.560
After all, that's why we go to movies like
"John Wick."

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:51.599
Audiences want to see badasses be badasses
and kill people in increasingly violent and

00:07:51.599 --> 00:07:52.610
clever ways.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:56.160
However, movies, even over-the-top action
films, need a sense of stakes.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.909
How is it, then, that John Wick seems relatively
unharmed from so many falls?

00:08:00.909 --> 00:08:02.220
It's not one time either.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.849
You could chalk up his survival in "John Wick:
Chapter 3 — Parabellum" to dumb luck and

00:08:05.849 --> 00:08:08.919
perseverance, but it happens a couple of times
in "John Wick: Chapter 4."

00:08:08.919 --> 00:08:13.430
We don't mind when this happens to say, Batman
or a "Looney Tunes" character, because surviving

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:15.389
great falls makes sense for them.

00:08:15.389 --> 00:08:19.020
It's not so easy to brush off in the "John
Wick" series, though, where gunshots, stab

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.520
wounds, and car crashes all do real damage.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.169
The only way it'd make sense is if Wick turns
out to have superpowers, or is secretly a

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:26.750
cartoon after all.

00:08:26.991 --> 00:08:30.011
The "John Wick" films obviously aren't going
for realism.

00:08:30.011 --> 00:08:34.430
First and foremost, these are over-the-top
action films, where one man can take on seemingly

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:38.040
hundreds of goons and only get slightly winded
by the end of it.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.650
Audiences are always going to be expected
to suspend their disbelief to enjoy a film

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:42.650
like this.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:47.879
One of the influences of "John Wick" is the
spy flick, where suspension of disbelief is paramount.

00:08:47.879 --> 00:08:52.209
This is why we see John wear a cool suit,
practice all manner of martial arts, and use

00:08:52.209 --> 00:08:53.209
specialized tech.

00:08:53.209 --> 00:08:57.160
In this case, his tech includes his impenetrable
black kevlar suit jacket.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:58.530
"And what style?"

00:08:58.530 --> 00:08:59.160
"Italian."

00:08:59.432 --> 00:09:00.432
"How many buttons?"

00:09:00.432 --> 00:09:01.160
"Two."

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:02.160
"Trousers?"

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.160
"Tapered."

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:04.160
"How about the lining?"

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:05.947
"Tactical."

00:09:05.947 --> 00:09:09.760
It's fun action-movie logic that a slick,
tailored suit can be made of kevlar and still

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:10.890
be lithe and mobile.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:13.510
However, why doesn't it cause recoil from
bullet hits?

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:17.290
It would, on a stunt and narrative level,
make the fights more interesting if it didn't

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:20.570
feel that John had practically a force field
protecting him in every fight.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:24.320
The shoot-out scenes are still awesome and
fun, but the film does lose a sense of danger

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:25.550
in many of the fights.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:29.561
Still, for all our complaints about the kevlar
suit, Reeves makes it look good.

00:09:30.269 --> 00:09:34.090
"John Wick: Chapter 4" isn't a spiritual or
supernatural film, and neither were any of

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:35.360
the other films in the series.

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:39.570
In fact, at one point, while Caine and Wick
both reminisce in a church before their duel,

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:41.110
Caine asks John a pointed question

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:44.150
"Do you think your wife can hear you?"

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:45.000
"No."

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:48.870
So it's surprising at the end that while John
slumps on the steps of the church, bleeding

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:52.888
to death from his injuries and Caine's bullet,
he sees visions of his late wife, Helen.

00:09:52.888 --> 00:09:54.670
"Maybe I'm wrong."

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:58.209
And given all the religious imagery throughout
the film, such as the final duel happening

00:09:58.209 --> 00:10:02.430
at a church and all the religious terminology
the assassin underworld speaks throughout

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:06.089
the franchise, it seems like there could be
a religious connotation to Wick's death after all.

00:10:06.089 --> 00:10:10.240
By sacrificing his life to save Caine's daughter,
he is absolved of his past sins to live in

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:11.399
eternity with his beloved.

00:10:11.399 --> 00:10:15.290
Of course, the most likely answer for these
intercut scenes of his wife is that John's

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:16.519
just remembering her.

00:10:16.519 --> 00:10:19.930
They're simply the last things he's thinking
about as he finally dies.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:21.159
But who can really say?

